## Solution

**Setup.** The surface is $\mathbf{q}(s,\theta)=(r(s)\cos\theta,\, r(s)\sin\theta,\, z(s))$ with $r>0$, $\dot z>0$, and the profile curve unit-speed: $\dot r^2+\dot z^2=1$. A latitude is the curve $\theta\mapsto \mathbf{q}(s_0,\theta)$ at fixed $s=s_0$.

**First fundamental form.** The tangent vectors are
$$\mathbf{q}_s=(\dot r\cos\theta,\dot r\sin\theta,\dot z),\qquad \mathbf{q}_\theta=(-r\sin\theta,r\cos\theta,0).$$
Using $\dot r^2+\dot z^2=1$,
$$E=\mathbf{q}_s\cdot\mathbf{q}_s=1,\quad F=\mathbf{q}_s\cdot\mathbf{q}_\theta=0,\quad G=\mathbf{q}_\theta\cdot\mathbf{q}_\theta=r^2.$$

**Unit normal.**
$$\mathbf{q}_s\times\mathbf{q}_\theta=(-\dot z\,r\cos\theta,\,-\dot z\,r\sin\theta,\,\dot r\,r),\qquad |\mathbf{q}_s\times\mathbf{q}_\theta|=r\sqrt{\dot z^2+\dot r^2}=r,$$
so
$$\mathbf{N}=(-\dot z\cos\theta,\,-\dot z\sin\theta,\,\dot r).$$

**Second fundamental form.** With
$$\mathbf{q}_{ss}=(\ddot r\cos\theta,\ddot r\sin\theta,\ddot z),\quad \mathbf{q}_{s\theta}=(-\dot r\sin\theta,\dot r\cos\theta,0),\quad \mathbf{q}_{\theta\theta}=(-r\cos\theta,-r\sin\theta,0),$$
$$e=\mathbf{q}_{ss}\cdot\mathbf{N}=-\dot z\,\ddot r+\dot r\,\ddot z.$$
Differentiating $\dot r^2+\dot z^2=1$ gives $\dot r\,\ddot r+\dot z\,\ddot z=0$, i.e. $\ddot z=-\dot r\,\ddot r/\dot z$. Hence
$$e=-\dot z\,\ddot r-\frac{\dot r^2\ddot r}{\dot z}=-\frac{\ddot r}{\dot z}(\dot z^2+\dot r^2)=-\frac{\ddot r}{\dot z},$$
$$f=\mathbf{q}_{s\theta}\cdot\mathbf{N}=\dot r\dot z\sin\theta\cos\theta-\dot r\dot z\sin\theta\cos\theta=0,$$
$$g=\mathbf{q}_{\theta\theta}\cdot\mathbf{N}=r\dot z\cos^2\theta+r\dot z\sin^2\theta=r\dot z.$$

Since $F=f=0$, the coordinate directions are **principal directions**, with principal curvatures
$$k_1=\frac{e}{E}=-\frac{\ddot r}{\dot z}\quad(\text{meridian}),\qquad k_2=\frac{g}{G}=\frac{\dot z}{r}\quad(\text{latitude}).$$

**The latitude curve.** Fix $s$; arc length along the latitude is $\sigma=r\theta$ (constant speed $r$). The unit tangent is
$$\mathbf{T}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_\theta}{r}=(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,0),\qquad \frac{d\mathbf{T}}{d\sigma}=\frac{1}{r}\frac{d\mathbf{T}}{d\theta}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{\theta\theta}}{r^2}=\Bigl(-\frac{\cos\theta}{r},-\frac{\sin\theta}{r},0\Bigr).$$
The (space) curvature is $\kappa=\bigl|\frac{d\mathbf{T}}{d\sigma}\bigr|=\frac{1}{r}$.

**Normal curvature.**
$$\kappa_n=\frac{d\mathbf{T}}{d\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{N}=\frac{\dot z\cos^2\theta+\dot z\sin^2\theta}{r}=\frac{\dot z}{r}.$$
This equals $k_2$, confirming the latitude runs along a principal direction.

**Geodesic curvature.** Using $\kappa^2=\kappa_g^2+\kappa_n^2$ (Pythagoras on the curvature vector decomposed into tangent and normal parts of the surface):
$$\kappa_g^2=\frac{1}{r^2}-\frac{\dot z^2}{r^2}=\frac{1-\dot z^2}{r^2}=\frac{\dot r^2}{r^2},\qquad \kappa_g=\frac{\dot r}{r}.$$
The sign is fixed by the standard convention $\kappa_g=\frac{d\mathbf{T}}{d\sigma}\cdot(\mathbf{N}\times\mathbf{T})$:
$$\mathbf{N}\times\mathbf{T}=(-\dot r\cos\theta,-\dot r\sin\theta,-\dot z),$$
$$\kappa_g=\Bigl(-\frac{\cos\theta}{r},-\frac{\sin\theta}{r},0\Bigr)\cdot(-\dot r\cos\theta,-\dot r\sin\theta,-\dot z)=\frac{\dot r}{r}.$$

**Geodesic torsion.** The latitude's tangent is along the principal direction $\mathbf{q}_\theta$. By Euler's formula the geodesic torsion of a direction making angle $\varphi$ with a principal direction is $\tau_g=(k_1-k_2)\sin\varphi\cos\varphi$. Here $\varphi=0$, hence
$$\tau_g=0.$$
(Equivalently, $\tau_g=\frac{fG-gF}{EG-F^2}=0$ since $f=F=0$.)

**Result.**
$$\boxed{\kappa_g=\frac{\dot r}{r},\qquad \kappa_n=\frac{\dot z}{r},\qquad \tau_g=0.}$$

Sanity check on the unit sphere $r=\cos s,\ z=\sin s$: $\kappa_n=\cos s/\cos s=1$ (correct, all directions have normal curvature $1$), $\kappa_g=-\sin s/\cos s=-\tan s$ (vanishes at the equator $s=0$, the only latitude that is a geodesic — correct), and $\tau_g=0$ (umbilic surface — correct).

### PROOF COMPLETE
